        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12314482/comment/12322140",
          "id": "12322140",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=cutting%40apache.org",
            "name": "cutting@apache.org",
            "key": "cutting@apache.org",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?avatarId=10453",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&avatarId=10453",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&avatarId=10453",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&avatarId=10453"
            },
            "displayName": "cutting@apache.org",
            "active": false,
            "timeZone": "Etc/UTC"
          },
          "body": "I think the plan (correct me if I'm wrong) is to fold the most useful of the\nsandbox items into the standard Lucene build tree when Lucene's code is moved to\na top-level project.  They'll still be packaged into separate jar file(s).\n\nOnce this happens, javadocs could either be built in a separate tree, or,\nperhaps better yet, put them in separate javadoc group(s):\n\nhttp://java.sun.com/j2se/1.4.2/docs/tooldocs/windows/javadoc.html#group\n\nFor example, Nutch uses groups to separate the Core \nAPIs from plugins:\n\nhttp://www.nutch.org/docs/api/overview-summary.html\n",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=cutting%40apache.org",
            "name": "cutting@apache.org",
            "key": "cutting@apache.org",
